= Dispersed knowledge — Simple =

'''Dispersed knowledge''' means that useful knowledge is spread across different people or places instead of being completely available to one decision-maker.

A warehouse worker may know that a particular batch is damaged. A planner may know future demand. A truck driver may know that a road is closed. No single person automatically knows all three facts.

The challenge is to make good decisions when the relevant knowledge is incomplete and distributed.

== Hayek's idea ==

Economist Friedrich Hayek made the concept especially influential in “The Use of Knowledge in Society” (1945). He argued that economic decisions depend on knowledge that is often local, temporary, and impossible to assume is already collected in one place.<ref name="hayek">[https://www.econlib.org/library/Essays/hykKnw.html Hayek, F. A. (1945), “The Use of Knowledge in Society.”]</ref>

He emphasized knowledge of the “circumstances of time and place”: facts known by people close to a particular situation.

== Prices can coordinate some local knowledge ==

Hayek argued that market prices can help coordinate decentralized decisions.

If a material becomes scarce and its price rises, users may conserve it or switch to substitutes without knowing the exact cause of the shortage.

The price communicates something useful, but it does not communicate everything.

Prices may fail to include pollution, unequal bargaining power, public benefits, fraud, or other effects that are not priced correctly.

So dispersed knowledge does **not** prove that markets always solve every coordination problem.

== Organizations have the same problem ==

Knowledge is also dispersed inside companies, governments, laboratories, and other organizations.

Different workers have different skills and local experience. Managers cannot always know in advance which practical detail will become important.

Haridimos Tsoukas described firms as distributed knowledge systems in which no single person has a complete view of everything the organization knows.<ref name="tsoukas">[https://doi.org/10.1002/smj.4250171104 Tsoukas, H. (1996), “The firm as a distributed knowledge system.”]</ref>

== Why knowledge becomes dispersed ==

Knowledge can be spread out because of:

* specialization;
* geography;
* changing local conditions;
* limited access;
* tacit skill;
* different interpretations;
* new discoveries.

Sometimes the problem is not that information is missing from a database. Nobody may yet know which information will matter.

== Ways to coordinate dispersed knowledge ==

Different systems use different tools:

* prices;
* managers and hierarchy;
* delegation;
* teams;
* standards;
* shared databases;
* expert networks;
* [[Knowledge market|knowledge markets]];
* communities of practice.

A good design often combines several of them.

== Centralized versus decentralized decisions ==

Centralized decisions can improve consistency and coordination.

Decentralized decisions can use local knowledge faster.

Neither is always better.

A practical organization might centralize security rules but let local teams make operational decisions. The correct balance depends on the task, communication costs, incentives, and risk.

== More information is not always enough ==

Markus Becker distinguishes between missing information and deeper ambiguity.<ref name="becker">[https://doi.org/10.1111/1467-6486.00271 Becker, M. C. (2001), “Managing Dispersed Knowledge.”]</ref>

If everyone agrees about the problem but one fact is missing, collecting more data may solve it.

If people disagree about what the problem even is, simply adding more data may not help. They may need interpretation, experimentation, or discussion.

